"Decomposes a project's final result into the products and component deliverables that must exist for acceptance.\n\nUse when: Agree what will be delivered before identifying the work. Clarify product composition and acceptance responsibilities.\n\nAvoid when: The audience needs task durations, precedence or resource allocation.\n\nVisual grammar: Product nouns label the root and component nodes. Parent-child links mean consists of rather than happens before. Product descriptions define quality and acceptance expectations separately.\n\nBuild: Name the final product and define its boundary. Break it into required component products and enabling deliverables. Write acceptance expectations and check for missing integration products.\n\nCheck: Every child is a product or deliverable rather than an action. The breakdown covers the final result at the chosen scope. External supplied products are identified rather than silently omitted.\n\nPitfalls: Turning product names into an activity to-do list. Assuming product composition determines the build sequence automatically.\n\nExample: A public exhibition is decomposed into display content, physical installations, visitor materials and an accepted operating package.\n\nAlternatives: Work breakdown structure, House of Quality."
